1
00:02:21,591 --> 00:02:26,392
The first time I ever saw a movie
at the Cinémathèque Française, I thought:

2
00:02:26,462 --> 00:02:31,923
"Only the French. Only the French
would house a cinema inside a palace".

3
00:02:42,612 --> 00:02:45,911
<i>The movie was Sam Fuller's
Shock Corridor.</i>

4
00:02:45,982 --> 00:02:51,147
Its images were so powerful,
it was like being hypnotized.

5
00:02:52,388 --> 00:02:58,327
I was 20 years old. It was the late '60s, and
I'd come to Paris for a year to study French.

6
00:02:58,394 --> 00:03:01,693
But it was here that I got my real education.

7
00:03:02,699 --> 00:03:07,033
I became a member of what in those days
was kind of a Freemasonry -

8
00:03:07,103 --> 00:03:09,594
the Freemasonry of cinephiles.

9
00:03:09,672 --> 00:03:12,368
What we'd call "film buffs".

10
00:03:19,215 --> 00:03:25,211
I was one of the insatiables. The ones you'd
always find sitting closest to the screen.

11
00:03:26,489 --> 00:03:29,253
Why did we sit so close?

12
00:03:29,325 --> 00:03:32,783
Maybe it was because
we wanted to receive the images first,

13
00:03:32,862 --> 00:03:36,059
when they were still new, still fresh.

14
00:03:36,132 --> 00:03:39,431
Before they cleared
the hurdles of the rows behind us.

15
00:03:39,502 --> 00:03:44,235
Before they had been relayed back
from row to row, spectator to spectator,

16
00:03:44,307 --> 00:03:49,870
until, worn out, the size of a postage stamp,
they returned to the projectionist's cabin.

17
00:03:53,182 --> 00:03:56,583
Maybe, too, the screen really was a screen.

18
00:03:56,653 --> 00:03:59,383
It screened us from the world.

19
00:04:10,767 --> 00:04:15,204
But there was one evening,
in the spring of 1968,

20
00:04:15,271 --> 00:04:19,037
when the world
finally burst through the screen.

21
00:04:19,108 --> 00:04:22,839
The pawn of an obscure coalition
of dubious interests,

22
00:04:22,912 --> 00:04:25,107
the Minister Malraux has driven

23
00:04:25,181 --> 00:04:28,981
Henri Langlois
out of the Cinémathèque Française.

24
00:04:29,052 --> 00:04:32,021
It offered us all
a free and fair conception

25
00:04:32,088 --> 00:04:34,181
of film culture.

26
00:04:34,257 --> 00:04:37,954
Now, for bureaucratic reasons,
culture's arch enemies

27
00:04:38,027 --> 00:04:40,552
have seized this bastion of liberty.

28
00:04:42,131 --> 00:04:43,530
Resist them!

29
00:04:43,599 --> 00:04:46,534
- Liberty isn't given!
- It's taken!

30
00:04:46,602 --> 00:04:48,035
All who love film...

31
00:04:48,104 --> 00:04:49,969
- in France...
- and abroad...

32
00:04:50,039 --> 00:04:51,028
<i>...are with you.</i>

33
00:04:51,107 --> 00:04:53,905
And with Henri Langlois!

34
00:05:03,252 --> 00:05:06,449
It was Henri Langlois
who created the Cinémathèque,

35
00:05:06,522 --> 00:05:11,425
because he liked to show movies instead of
letting them rot in some underground vault.

36
00:05:11,494 --> 00:05:14,554
He'd show any movies - good, bad, old, new.

37
00:05:14,630 --> 00:05:17,394
Silents, Westerns, thrillers.

38
00:05:17,467 --> 00:05:22,461
And all the New Wave filmmakers
came here to learn their craft.

39
00:05:22,538 --> 00:05:25,598
This is where modern cinema was born.

40
00:05:25,675 --> 00:05:27,939
What lies behind it:

41
00:05:28,011 --> 00:05:30,275
The police!

42
00:05:31,714 --> 00:05:34,148
Langlois had been sacked
by the government,

43
00:05:34,217 --> 00:05:38,813
and it seemed like every film buff
in Paris had turned out in protest.

44
00:05:38,888 --> 00:05:41,413
It was our very own cultural revolution.

45
00:06:06,516 --> 00:06:08,507
Excuse me.

46
00:06:11,120 --> 00:06:13,281
Can you remove this?

47
00:06:13,356 --> 00:06:15,483
It's stuck to my lips.

48
00:06:16,192 --> 00:06:19,491
Can you remove my cigarette? It's stuck.

49
00:06:19,562 --> 00:06:21,757
Yes, yes...

50
00:06:21,831 --> 00:06:23,355
Of course.

51
00:06:25,001 --> 00:06:26,935
Sorry.

52
00:06:27,937 --> 00:06:30,929
- What are you? English?
- No, I'm American.

53
00:06:31,007 --> 00:06:33,498
You can put it out now.

54
00:06:36,913 --> 00:06:39,381
- What's your name?
- Matthew.

55
00:06:39,449 --> 00:06:42,418
You're here a lot.
But you never talk to anyone.

56
00:06:42,485 --> 00:06:44,885
We've been wondering
why you're always alone.

57
00:06:44,954 --> 00:06:47,479
I don't really know anybody.

58
00:06:48,491 --> 00:06:53,952
- How come you're chained to the gates?
- I'm not chained to the gates.

59
00:06:56,265 --> 00:06:58,995
- You're awfully clean.
- What?

60
00:06:59,068 --> 00:07:01,628
For someone who likes the cinema so much.

61
00:07:01,704 --> 00:07:03,763
- Do you know Jacques?
- Jacques?

62
00:07:03,840 --> 00:07:06,968
If shit could shit,
it would smell just like Jacques.

63
00:07:07,043 --> 00:07:11,139
My brother's gone to talk to him. When
he gets back, you'll smell Jacques on him.

64
00:07:11,214 --> 00:07:13,842
It's like being around pigs.

65
00:07:14,984 --> 00:07:17,350
- You speak English really well.
- What?

66
00:07:17,420 --> 00:07:21,447
- You speak English really well.
- My mother's English.

67
00:07:21,524 --> 00:07:25,620
- Here's Theo. When I introduce you, sniff.
- Come on.

68
00:07:25,695 --> 00:07:28,220
Truffaut, Godard, Chabrol, Rivette,

69
00:07:28,297 --> 00:07:31,789
and Renoir, Jean Rouch,
Rohmer are here.

70
00:07:31,868 --> 00:07:34,666
Signoret, Jean Marais
and God knows who else.

71
00:07:34,737 --> 00:07:35,965
Marcel Carné too.

72
00:07:36,038 --> 00:07:37,733
Why is Carné here?

73
00:07:37,807 --> 00:07:41,174
What should we do?
Stay or leave?

74
00:07:41,244 --> 00:07:43,542
I don't know.

75
00:07:43,613 --> 00:07:44,875
Theo.

76
00:07:44,947 --> 00:07:47,438
This is Matthew.

77
00:07:47,517 --> 00:07:50,042
- You were right - he's American.
- Hi.

78
00:07:52,989 --> 00:07:56,823
I've seen you around. You've been
coming to all the Nicholas Rays.

79
00:07:56,893 --> 00:07:59,384
Yeah. I really like his movies.

80
00:07:59,462 --> 00:08:02,056
<i>What? They Live by Night?</i>

81
00:08:02,131 --> 00:08:07,501
<i>More like Johnny Guitar
and Rebel Without a Cause.</i>

82
00:08:07,570 --> 00:08:10,937
- You know what Godard wrote about him?
- No, what?

83
00:08:11,007 --> 00:08:13,271
"Nicholas Ray is cinema".

84
00:08:14,310 --> 00:08:16,335
- What's up with you?
- Me?

85
00:08:16,412 --> 00:08:18,710
Come on, stick with me!

86
00:08:51,447 --> 00:08:52,778
Fascists!

87
00:08:52,848 --> 00:08:55,316
- Bastards!
- Assholes!

88
00:08:58,988 --> 00:09:03,220
And that was how I first met
Theo and Isabelle.

89
00:09:11,567 --> 00:09:14,195
I could hear my heart pounding.

90
00:09:14,270 --> 00:09:17,569
I don't know if it was because
I'd just been chased by the police

91
00:09:17,640 --> 00:09:20,871
or because I was already in love
with my new friends.

92
00:09:20,943 --> 00:09:26,848
As we walked and talked, and talked,
and talked - about politics, about movies,

93
00:09:26,916 --> 00:09:29,942
and why the French could never
produce a good rock band...

94
00:09:30,019 --> 00:09:32,886
- I'm starving.
- I forgot the sandwiches.

95
00:09:32,955 --> 00:09:35,856
<i>...I didn't want that night ever to end.</i>

96
00:09:39,695 --> 00:09:44,894
- Didn't you bring anything?
- No, I'm OK. Please, eat. Don't mind me.

97
00:09:46,402 --> 00:09:49,735
- No, I'm really not hungry.
- I've broken it now. Take it.

98
00:09:49,805 --> 00:09:53,332
- You're very kind, but I don't...
- Take it when you're offered it.

99
00:09:53,409 --> 00:09:55,070
Thank you.

100
00:09:55,144 --> 00:09:56,543
- Theo.
- What?

101
00:09:56,612 --> 00:09:59,240
- Don't you have something for Matthew?
- This is fine.

102
00:09:59,315 --> 00:10:02,409
- I gave him a third of mine.
- OK.

103
00:10:04,954 --> 00:10:08,117
I really... I didn't come here
to eat your sandwiches.

104
00:10:08,190 --> 00:10:10,988
- He doesn't want it.
- Yes, he does.

105
00:10:11,060 --> 00:10:15,053
- He's too polite to say he does, aren't you?
- You're both very kind.

106
00:10:15,131 --> 00:10:17,827
So, where are you from exactly?

107
00:10:17,900 --> 00:10:19,800
San Diego.

108
00:10:19,869 --> 00:10:23,805
What about you two?
Were you both born in Paris?

109
00:10:23,873 --> 00:10:28,401
I entered this world
on the Champs Elysées. 1959.

110
00:10:28,477 --> 00:10:30,672
The pavement
of the Champs Elysées.

111
00:10:30,746 --> 00:10:33,681
And you know
what my very first words were?

112
00:10:33,749 --> 00:10:37,879
- No. What?
- "New York Herald Tribune".

113
00:10:45,795 --> 00:10:48,628
Will you come to Rome with me?

114
00:10:52,935 --> 00:10:55,096
Here. Right here. Here.

115
00:10:55,171 --> 00:10:57,696
No, there!

116
00:10:57,773 --> 00:11:01,231
- Good night, Matthew.
- Night.

117
00:11:49,992 --> 00:11:52,085
Dear Mom,

118
00:11:52,528 --> 00:11:55,019
I got some real news this time.

119
00:11:56,632 --> 00:12:00,159
I've just met my first French friends.

120
00:12:44,346 --> 00:12:45,813
Hello?

121
00:12:45,881 --> 00:12:49,317
- Matthew?
- Who is this?

122
00:12:49,385 --> 00:12:52,548
- Don't be so suspicious. It's me.
- Theo?

123
00:12:52,621 --> 00:12:57,058
- Don't tell me I woke you up.
- No, I've been up for ages.

124
00:12:57,126 --> 00:12:59,424
You don't sound as if
you've been up for ages.

125
00:12:59,495 --> 00:13:01,622
I always sound like this in the morning.

126
00:13:01,697 --> 00:13:04,860
Sorry, I have to call you early
because I've got a class at nine.

127
00:13:04,934 --> 00:13:09,633
- Right.
- You want to have dinner tomorrow night?

128
00:13:13,309 --> 00:13:16,710
You mean like a real dinner
in a nice restaurant?

129
00:13:16,779 --> 00:13:19,942
No, no. Not in a restaurant. Here. At home.

130
00:13:20,716 --> 00:13:23,207
Yeah. Sure, that would be great.

131
00:13:23,285 --> 00:13:27,619
- Yes? OK. Why don't you...
- Please get off the phone. It's nine o'clock.

132
00:13:27,690 --> 00:13:31,786
Yes, yes. Why don't you meet us
for a drink first? At the Respite.

133
00:13:31,861 --> 00:13:35,058
Six o'clock. You know where it is?

134
00:13:35,130 --> 00:13:38,031
- Boulevard Saint-Germain?
- Be there at six.

135
00:13:46,842 --> 00:13:49,572
Third floor!

136
00:13:49,645 --> 00:13:52,011
You know, there's room in here for all of us.

137
00:13:52,081 --> 00:13:54,242
Theo and I are contagious.

138
00:13:55,217 --> 00:13:58,778
- We are very contagious.
- Contagious?

139
00:13:58,854 --> 00:14:01,254
You mustn't catch us.

140
00:14:19,141 --> 00:14:21,939
- It's beautiful.
- You think so?

141
00:14:22,011 --> 00:14:24,002
Good luck.

142
00:14:32,187 --> 00:14:34,178
Evening, mother.

143
00:14:35,958 --> 00:14:37,755
What are you doing here?

144
00:14:37,826 --> 00:14:39,726
- We're here for dinner.
- What?

145
00:14:39,795 --> 00:14:41,763
With Matthew.

146
00:14:41,830 --> 00:14:44,355
- Didn't Isabelle say?
- Which Matthew?

147
00:14:44,433 --> 00:14:47,561
This Matthew. He's our new friend.

148
00:14:47,636 --> 00:14:50,469
- Matthew, this is my mother.
- Hi.

149
00:14:50,539 --> 00:14:53,872
- Very pleased to meet you, Matthew.
- Likewise.

150
00:14:53,943 --> 00:14:57,879
- You're American, are you?
- Yeah, I'm from California.

151
00:14:57,947 --> 00:15:03,977
Matthew lives in a nasty student hotel.
Rue Malebranche. So we invited him to dinner.

152
00:15:04,053 --> 00:15:05,020
What's wrong?

153
00:15:05,087 --> 00:15:08,420
What can I say, Isabelle?
I'm cooking for two

154
00:15:08,490 --> 00:15:10,151
and now there are five of us.

155
00:15:10,225 --> 00:15:11,453
Didn't Theo tell you?

156
00:15:11,527 --> 00:15:14,155
No, he didn't. And neither did you.

157
00:15:14,229 --> 00:15:17,323
Have you got mashed potatoes
for brains or what?

158
00:15:17,399 --> 00:15:21,165
- You were supposed to tell her!
- You're crazy!

159
00:15:21,236 --> 00:15:23,500
Bitch, cow, slut...

160
00:15:23,572 --> 00:15:26,735
For God's sake, put a sock in it, you two.

161
00:15:29,078 --> 00:15:32,047
I must apologize
for my children's behavior, Matthew.

162
00:15:32,114 --> 00:15:35,447
No, I'm the one who should be apologizing.

163
00:15:35,517 --> 00:15:37,951
Please, don't put yourself out
on account of me.

164
00:15:38,020 --> 00:15:40,750
That's sweet of you.
You're completely blameless.

165
00:15:44,193 --> 00:15:46,388
Let me look at you.

166
00:15:47,129 --> 00:15:50,895
OK. I want you to make
a good impression on Papa.

167
00:16:02,211 --> 00:16:03,769
Papa, it's me.

168
00:16:03,846 --> 00:16:05,746
We're eating in.

169
00:16:05,814 --> 00:16:07,475
And the Cinémathèque?

170
00:16:07,549 --> 00:16:09,813
Closed until further notice.

171
00:16:09,885 --> 00:16:11,216
Papa!

172
00:16:11,286 --> 00:16:13,447
Can't you see we have a guest?

173
00:16:13,522 --> 00:16:15,786
This is Matthew.

174
00:16:17,092 --> 00:16:19,117
- Sir.
- Matthew.

175
00:16:19,194 --> 00:16:22,163
Matthew's dining with us tonight.

176
00:16:22,431 --> 00:16:26,561
My young Matthew, inspiration is like a baby.

177
00:16:26,635 --> 00:16:30,765
It does not choose a nice,
seemly hour to enter the world.

178
00:16:31,273 --> 00:16:35,209
It has no consideration for the poor poet.

179
00:16:36,578 --> 00:16:39,012
But when it does come,

180
00:16:39,081 --> 00:16:42,710
when it deigns to come,
then you know it's...

181
00:16:48,323 --> 00:16:53,124
Young man. I was speaking to you.
I imagined you were listening.

182
00:16:53,195 --> 00:16:55,527
I was. I'm sorry.

183
00:16:57,166 --> 00:16:58,827
What?

184
00:16:59,835 --> 00:17:02,633
Nothing. I was... I was just...

185
00:17:04,506 --> 00:17:09,808
You seem to be mesmerized
by this tin lighter. I'd like to know why.

186
00:17:12,114 --> 00:17:14,674
- Well?
- George, please. Matthew's our guest.

187
00:17:14,750 --> 00:17:19,346
No, I'm genuinely curious.
I'd like to know why.

188
00:17:22,791 --> 00:17:27,160
I was just... I was fidgeting
with Isabelle's lighter.

189
00:17:27,229 --> 00:17:30,255
I wasn't realizing it,
and I noticed and thought it was rude

190
00:17:30,332 --> 00:17:32,664
so I put it down on the table.

191
00:17:32,735 --> 00:17:37,570
But I put it diagonally across
one of these squares, do you see?

192
00:17:37,639 --> 00:17:39,630
Look.

193
00:17:39,708 --> 00:17:45,578
I noticed that the lighter's length is exactly
the same length as the diagonal itself.

194
00:17:45,647 --> 00:17:47,911
So I put it lengthwise.

195
00:17:48,917 --> 00:17:51,977
Along the outside edge. Look.

196
00:17:52,054 --> 00:17:54,352
- It fits there too.
- Yeah.

197
00:17:55,457 --> 00:17:58,119
But it fits there.

198
00:17:58,794 --> 00:18:02,195
And it fits like this. And like this.

199
00:18:02,898 --> 00:18:05,526
And this way, too.

200
00:18:05,601 --> 00:18:08,900
I bet you if I just split it in half,

201
00:18:09,738 --> 00:18:11,865
it's gotta fit somewhere.

202
00:18:12,908 --> 00:18:16,639
I mean, it really fits anywhere. Look.

203
00:18:16,712 --> 00:18:18,509
You see?

204
00:18:18,580 --> 00:18:22,141
I was noticing that
the more you look at everything -

205
00:18:22,217 --> 00:18:25,618
this table, the objects on it, the refrigerator,

206
00:18:25,687 --> 00:18:27,780
this room, your nose,

207
00:18:27,856 --> 00:18:30,222
the world -

208
00:18:30,292 --> 00:18:34,524
suddenly, you realize that

209
00:18:34,596 --> 00:18:37,827
there's some sort of cosmic harmony

210
00:18:37,900 --> 00:18:40,494
of shapes and sizes.

211
00:18:43,005 --> 00:18:45,235
I was just wondering why.

212
00:18:45,307 --> 00:18:47,571
I don't know why that is.

213
00:18:47,643 --> 00:18:50,111
I know that it is.

214
00:18:56,852 --> 00:18:59,514
You have an interesting friend here.

215
00:18:59,588 --> 00:19:03,024
More interesting, I suspect, than you know.

216
00:19:05,460 --> 00:19:10,124
I mean, when we look around us,
what is it we see?

217
00:19:12,134 --> 00:19:15,001
Complete chaos.

218
00:19:15,070 --> 00:19:17,265
Yet, viewed from above -

219
00:19:17,339 --> 00:19:20,672
viewed, as it were, by God -

220
00:19:20,742 --> 00:19:23,609
everything suddenly fits together.

221
00:19:26,215 --> 00:19:32,916
My children believe that their demonstrations
and sit-ins and happenings...

222
00:19:33,956 --> 00:19:39,258
Well, they believe that these possess
the capacity not only to provoke society,

223
00:19:39,328 --> 00:19:41,819
but also to transform it.

224
00:19:46,969 --> 00:19:51,702
What is it you are saying? If Langlois
is dismissed we shouldn't do anything?

225
00:19:51,773 --> 00:19:56,540
If immigrants are deported, if students
are beaten up, we shouldn't do anything?

226
00:19:56,612 --> 00:20:00,810
All I am saying is
that a little lucidity would not go amiss.

227
00:20:00,883 --> 00:20:03,818
So everyone's wrong but you?

228
00:20:04,887 --> 00:20:07,583
In France? In Italy? Germany? America?

229
00:20:07,656 --> 00:20:13,595
Listen to me. Before you can change the
world, you must realize you are part of it.

230
00:20:13,662 --> 00:20:17,098
You cannot stand outside, looking in.

231
00:20:17,165 --> 00:20:20,100
You're the one who stands outside.

232
00:20:20,168 --> 00:20:23,331
You refused to sign a petition
against the Vietnam war.

233
00:20:23,405 --> 00:20:26,670
Poets don't sign petitions, they sign poems.

234
00:20:27,409 --> 00:20:30,435
- A petition is a poem.
- Yes.

235
00:20:30,512 --> 00:20:33,811
And a poem is a petition.
Thank you, but I'm not gaga yet.

236
00:20:33,882 --> 00:20:37,113
I don't need you
to remind me of my own work.

237
00:20:37,853 --> 00:20:39,411
That's right.

238
00:20:39,488 --> 00:20:42,480
A petition is a poem,
a poem is a petition.

239
00:20:42,557 --> 00:20:48,018
- Yes.
- The most famous lines you ever wrote.

240
00:20:49,097 --> 00:20:51,429
Now look at you.

241
00:20:53,568 --> 00:20:56,298
I hope I'll never be like him.

242
00:20:57,706 --> 00:21:03,406
We should say good night, darling.
We've got a long day ahead of us tomorrow.

243
00:21:03,478 --> 00:21:05,036
George.

244
00:21:05,113 --> 00:21:07,513
Yes, sorry. You were saying?

245
00:21:07,582 --> 00:21:09,015
We should get to bed.

246
00:21:11,520 --> 00:21:15,115
You had something to tell
the children, didn't you?

247
00:21:15,190 --> 00:21:16,851
Yes, of course.

248
00:21:16,925 --> 00:21:18,392
I'm sorry.

249
00:21:18,460 --> 00:21:20,792
The checks are on the mantelpiece.

250
00:21:20,862 --> 00:21:24,525
Don't cash them until
you need them, all right?

251
00:21:26,101 --> 00:21:28,661
Good night, everybody.

252
00:21:28,737 --> 00:21:31,535
Good night, papa.
Have a safe journey.

253
00:21:31,606 --> 00:21:33,233
Good night, dear.

254
00:21:33,308 --> 00:21:34,969
Theo.

255
00:21:36,278 --> 00:21:42,148
- A very good night to you, Matthew.
- It was a pleasure meeting you, sir.

256
00:21:42,217 --> 00:21:45,516
- Very nice meeting you.
- You too. Thank you so much for dinner.

257
00:21:45,587 --> 00:21:46,849
My pleasure.

258
00:21:46,922 --> 00:21:52,087
Why don't you spend the night? I'm not sure
I like the sound of Hotel Malebranche.

259
00:21:52,160 --> 00:21:54,151
Good night.

260
00:22:20,655 --> 00:22:25,422
Why haven't you dazzled us with these
philosophical speculations of yours?

261
00:22:25,494 --> 00:22:27,826
I didn't know I was being philosophical.

262
00:22:27,896 --> 00:22:32,492
- Papa was awfully impressed.
- Papa's full of shit.

263
00:22:32,567 --> 00:22:36,867
I think you're lucky.
I wish my parents were that nice.

264
00:22:36,938 --> 00:22:41,068
Other people's parents
are always nicer than our own.

265
00:22:41,143 --> 00:22:46,513
And yet our own grandparents
are always nicer than other people's.

266
00:22:48,216 --> 00:22:51,083
You know, that's true.
That's absolutely true.

267
00:22:51,153 --> 00:22:54,850
I never thought about it before, but it's true.

268
00:22:54,923 --> 00:22:57,153
Matthew, you're sweet.

269
00:22:59,261 --> 00:23:03,459
This is double the length of...

270
00:23:06,701 --> 00:23:09,033
I'm for bed.

271
00:23:21,349 --> 00:23:23,476
Are you staying?

272
00:23:24,486 --> 00:23:26,977
- Are you OK?
- Yeah, I'm OK.

273
00:23:28,457 --> 00:23:30,186
Good night, Matthew.

274
00:23:41,336 --> 00:23:43,998
Everybody's got a father.

275
00:23:44,072 --> 00:23:45,869
Yes.

276
00:23:45,941 --> 00:23:51,538
But the fact that God doesn't exist
doesn't mean he can take his place.

277
00:24:36,391 --> 00:24:37,881
OK?

278
00:24:40,462 --> 00:24:43,124
- Yeah, it's fine.
- Good night.

279
00:24:43,198 --> 00:24:45,325
- Good night.
- See you in the morning.

280
00:24:45,400 --> 00:24:48,267
See you. Thank...

281
00:24:48,336 --> 00:24:50,327
Thank you.

282
00:28:02,664 --> 00:28:04,825
Good morning.

283
00:28:07,635 --> 00:28:09,660
What was that all about?

284
00:28:09,738 --> 00:28:11,865
I was removing the sleep from your eyes.

285
00:28:11,940 --> 00:28:15,103
Theo lets me do his every morning.

286
00:28:16,111 --> 00:28:18,443
It's a strange thing to want to do.

287
00:28:18,513 --> 00:28:20,811
Didn't you enjoy it?

288
00:28:21,549 --> 00:28:24,450
- Was I supposed to?
- Naturally.

289
00:28:31,593 --> 00:28:32,958
Up.

290
00:28:33,027 --> 00:28:37,657
- What are you waiting for?
- Isabelle, I'm not dressed.

291
00:29:37,292 --> 00:29:38,884
What are you doing?

292
00:29:38,960 --> 00:29:44,921
- What are you doing?
- I've been memorizing this room.

293
00:29:44,999 --> 00:29:48,059
In the future, in my memory,

294
00:29:48,136 --> 00:29:52,129
I shall live a great deal in this room.

295
00:29:52,207 --> 00:29:54,107
<i>Queen Christina.</i>

296
00:29:54,175 --> 00:29:58,168
Where Garbo bids farewell to the room
in which she spent the night with Gilbert.

297
00:29:58,246 --> 00:30:00,976
Bravo!

298
00:30:01,049 --> 00:30:05,247
We have a private wing to ourselves.
The bathroom's at the end of the corridor.

299
00:30:05,320 --> 00:30:10,314
If you aren't there in one minute,
we're coming back to get you.

300
00:30:40,822 --> 00:30:42,813
Come in.

301
00:30:44,859 --> 00:30:47,020
Good morning.

302
00:30:47,095 --> 00:30:49,586
Are you going to church?

303
00:30:53,067 --> 00:30:56,434
Oh, sorry. You want to use my toothbrush?

304
00:30:56,504 --> 00:30:58,301
- No.
- Sure?

305
00:30:58,373 --> 00:31:00,705
I'll use my finger.

306
00:31:06,948 --> 00:31:09,815
- Sleep well?
- Yeah.

307
00:31:09,884 --> 00:31:12,216
I mean, fine.

308
00:31:22,497 --> 00:31:28,265
You know, Matthew, you really do
have the most beautiful pair of lips.

309
00:31:30,038 --> 00:31:32,632
Can I touch them?

310
00:31:33,641 --> 00:31:37,202
You wanna touch my lips?

311
00:31:37,278 --> 00:31:39,337
OK.

312
00:31:43,985 --> 00:31:45,976
They're so red,

313
00:31:46,054 --> 00:31:47,988
and ripe

314
00:31:48,056 --> 00:31:49,853
and luscious.

315
00:31:49,924 --> 00:31:52,085
So sullen.

316
00:31:52,160 --> 00:31:54,822
Brutal.

317
00:31:54,896 --> 00:31:58,059
- Let's see what you'd look like with lipstick.
- I should go.

318
00:31:58,132 --> 00:32:01,533
- You'd make such a pretty girl.
- Ignore her, Matthew.

319
00:32:01,603 --> 00:32:08,202
- She sucks in all the air around her.
- I really have to be getting back to my hotel.

320
00:32:08,276 --> 00:32:12,042
No. Why?
We were going to invite you to stay.

321
00:32:12,113 --> 00:32:13,944
- Really?
- Sure.

322
00:32:14,015 --> 00:32:17,314
Our parents are going to be
in Trouville for a month.

323
00:32:17,385 --> 00:32:21,344
We thought you might like
to move your things here.

324
00:32:21,422 --> 00:32:23,788
- Really?
- Sure.

325
00:32:23,858 --> 00:32:27,521
But you just met me two days ago.
You barely even know me.

326
00:32:27,595 --> 00:32:30,428
This way we get to know you.
Do you have to stay in the hotel?

327
00:32:30,498 --> 00:32:35,026
- No.
- We'll go and pick up what you need. OK?

328
00:32:35,103 --> 00:32:37,264
Come on.

329
00:32:37,338 --> 00:32:39,829
What about you?

330
00:32:42,310 --> 00:32:44,073
It was my idea.

331
00:32:45,480 --> 00:32:48,074
You're scaring me right now.

332
00:32:49,250 --> 00:32:51,445
Jesus.

333
00:32:51,619 --> 00:32:53,610
Jesus.

334
00:32:56,791 --> 00:32:59,487
I'm going to check out
the Cinémathèque, just in case.

335
00:32:59,560 --> 00:33:01,653
- OK.
- Don't take too long.

336
00:33:01,729 --> 00:33:07,065
- Don't worry. I won't. I don't have much stuff.
- With us, you're not going to need much.

337
00:33:13,174 --> 00:33:16,405
Just don't forget your toothbrush.

338
00:33:28,189 --> 00:33:33,286
Dear Mom, I guess you're surprised to be
hearing from me so soon after my last letter,

339
00:33:33,361 --> 00:33:38,355
but I've just moved into an apartment
belonging to a famous French author.

340
00:33:38,433 --> 00:33:41,800
His children are the same age as me
and have the same interests.

341
00:33:41,869 --> 00:33:46,431
I know how pleased you'll be to hear
I'm getting in with the right kind of people.

342
00:33:46,507 --> 00:33:49,442
Say hello to Father.
I hope he's not still mad at me...

343
00:33:49,744 --> 00:33:52,042
Listen to this, Matthew.

344
00:33:52,113 --> 00:33:57,312
"The difference between Keaton and Chaplin
is the difference between prose and poetry,

345
00:33:57,385 --> 00:34:02,322
between the aristocrat and the tramp,
between eccentricity and mysticism,

346
00:34:02,390 --> 00:34:06,884
between man as a machine
and man as angel".

347
00:34:06,961 --> 00:34:08,326
Not bad, huh?

348
00:34:08,396 --> 00:34:11,991
That's good. Except, for me,
there's no comparison.

349
00:34:12,533 --> 00:34:16,025
Why? Because Chaplin's incomparable?

350
00:34:17,905 --> 00:34:21,306
No. Because Keaton is incomparable.

351
00:34:22,210 --> 00:34:25,577
Keaton? You think Keaton's
greater than Chaplin?

352
00:34:25,646 --> 00:34:27,705
- Absolutely, I do.
- You're not serious.

353
00:34:27,782 --> 00:34:30,080
- Course I am.
- You're crazy.

354
00:34:30,151 --> 00:34:33,848
Come on. You can't deny
that Keaton's funnier than Chaplin.

355
00:34:33,921 --> 00:34:38,255
- Yes, I can.
- You don't find Keaton funnier than Chaplin?

356
00:34:38,326 --> 00:34:40,590
I don't think anyone's funnier than Chaplin.

357
00:34:40,661 --> 00:34:43,425
Keaton is. Even when he's
not doing anything he's funny.

358
00:34:43,498 --> 00:34:46,433
And he looks like Godard.
Keaton is a real filmmaker.

359
00:34:46,501 --> 00:34:50,369
Chaplin - all he cares about
is his own performance, his own ego.

360
00:34:50,438 --> 00:34:52,303
- Bullshit.
- It's not bullshit.

361
00:34:52,373 --> 00:34:57,333
Yes, it is. Sometimes I think you Americans
understand fuck all about your own culture.

362
00:34:57,411 --> 00:35:02,212
- No wonder you never got Jerry Lewis.
- Don't even get me started on Jerry Lewis.

363
00:35:02,283 --> 00:35:06,447
Listen, Matthew. When Chaplin wanted
to have a beautiful shot, he knew how.

364
00:35:06,521 --> 00:35:09,718
Better than Keaton. Better than anybody.

365
00:35:09,791 --> 00:35:12,089
<i>You remember the last shot of City Lights?</i>

366
00:35:12,160 --> 00:35:16,153
He looks at the flower girl, she looks at him.

367
00:35:16,764 --> 00:35:21,133
And don't forget she's been blind,
so she was seeing him for the very first time.

368
00:35:21,202 --> 00:35:25,536
And it's as if, through her eyes,
we also see him for the very first time.

369
00:35:25,606 --> 00:35:29,007
Charlie Chaplin, Charlot,
the most famous man in the world,

370
00:35:29,076 --> 00:35:32,944
and it's as if
we've never really seen him before.

371
00:35:38,619 --> 00:35:42,715
Isa, if you play that fucking record again,
I'll break it in two. OK?

372
00:35:42,790 --> 00:35:45,384
But you like Janis Joplin.

373
00:35:47,628 --> 00:35:49,061
Stop it.

374
00:35:49,130 --> 00:35:51,826
I said stop it, Isabelle.

375
00:35:52,633 --> 00:35:53,861
Stop!

376
00:35:53,935 --> 00:35:56,802
- Matthew, tell me - what film?
- What?

377
00:35:56,871 --> 00:36:01,535
Name a film where someone tap-dancing
drives someone else crazy.

378
00:36:01,609 --> 00:36:03,770
- I know this.
- Forfeit if you can't answer.

379
00:36:03,845 --> 00:36:07,281
<i>- Quick, quick or you'll pay the forfeit.
- Top Hat. Top Hat.</i>

380
00:36:07,348 --> 00:36:11,375
Fred Astaire is dancing
over Ginger Rogers' room.

381
00:36:13,588 --> 00:36:16,580
And she's mad because he wakes her up.

382
00:36:22,530 --> 00:36:24,327
I'm right.

383
00:36:25,766 --> 00:36:26,892
He's good.

384
00:36:26,968 --> 00:36:29,698
You thinking what I'm thinking?

385
00:36:29,770 --> 00:36:31,203
What?

386
00:36:31,272 --> 00:36:32,864
<i>Bande à part.</i>

387
00:36:32,940 --> 00:36:34,931
You're right.

388
00:36:35,142 --> 00:36:38,407
Why are you two looking at me like that?

389
00:36:38,479 --> 00:36:40,276
Well,

390
00:36:40,348 --> 00:36:42,714
you see, my little Matthew,

391
00:36:42,783 --> 00:36:46,082
there's something Theo and I
have been meaning to do for a long time,

392
00:36:46,153 --> 00:36:49,020
but we've been waiting
for the right person to do it with.

393
00:36:49,090 --> 00:36:51,718
- And I think you're finally it.
- To do what?

394
00:36:51,792 --> 00:36:55,091
<i>- Try to beat the record of Bande à part.
- What record?</i>

395
00:36:55,162 --> 00:36:57,221
<i>- You've seen Bande à part?
- Yeah.</i>

396
00:36:57,298 --> 00:37:01,291
- Where they race through the Louvre?
- Remind me.

397
00:37:01,369 --> 00:37:04,133
To beat the world record
of nine minutes and 45 seconds.

398
00:37:04,205 --> 00:37:07,766
- Oh, yeah. Of course.
- We're going to beat their record.

399
00:37:11,212 --> 00:37:14,204
- What?
- What's the matter?

400
00:37:14,615 --> 00:37:17,345
- Nothing, really.
- Not afraid, are you?

401
00:37:17,418 --> 00:37:21,013
- No. I'm not afraid.
- What, then?

402
00:37:21,088 --> 00:37:24,854
It's easy for you two. I'm an American,
what you French call an "alien".

403
00:37:24,926 --> 00:37:28,157
- So?
- So if I get caught, I get deported.

404
00:37:28,229 --> 00:37:31,426
Don't worry, little man.
We're not going to get caught.

405
00:37:31,499 --> 00:37:34,229
<i>- You don't know that.
- They weren't caught in Bande à part.</i>

406
00:37:34,302 --> 00:37:37,328
- If we beat the record, we won't be caught.
- It's a movie.

407
00:37:37,405 --> 00:37:39,566
Go get Papa's stopwatch. Come on.

408
00:37:39,640 --> 00:37:44,543
- It's a great idea...
- Matthew, this is a test.

409
00:37:44,612 --> 00:37:48,275
Are you going to pass it or fail it?

410
00:37:48,349 --> 00:37:50,112
Be careful.

411
00:37:50,184 --> 00:37:52,914
A lot depends on how you answer.

412
00:38:26,087 --> 00:38:31,115
Nine minutes and 28 seconds.
We beat the record by 17 seconds.

413
00:38:33,327 --> 00:38:36,194
Matthew. My little Matthew.

414
00:38:36,263 --> 00:38:38,754
You were wonderful.

415
00:38:42,837 --> 00:38:45,067
We accept him. One of us.

416
00:38:45,239 --> 00:38:48,436
We accept him. One of us.

417
00:38:48,509 --> 00:38:51,808
One of us. We accept her, we accept her.

418
00:38:55,983 --> 00:38:57,780
We accept him. One of us.

419
00:38:57,852 --> 00:38:58,944
One of us.

420
00:39:45,466 --> 00:39:51,427
We accept you. One of us.
One of us. One of us. One of us. One of us.

421
00:39:57,344 --> 00:39:59,335
That's Mother.

422
00:40:01,315 --> 00:40:03,476
I'm too wet to answer.

423
00:40:07,755 --> 00:40:10,690
Isa's smart, but she doesn't know
how to deal with parents.

424
00:40:10,758 --> 00:40:15,058
- What do you mean?
- I mean, it's not enough to ignore them.

425
00:40:15,129 --> 00:40:19,896
They should all be arrested. Put on trial.

426
00:40:19,967 --> 00:40:22,299
Confess their crimes.

427
00:40:22,369 --> 00:40:28,001
Sent to the country
for self-criticism and re-education.

428
00:40:29,243 --> 00:40:33,873
- They're already in the country.
- They are at the seaside.

429
00:40:33,948 --> 00:40:35,939
That's different.

430
00:40:41,589 --> 00:40:42,954
Too bad.

431
00:40:43,023 --> 00:40:45,583
My parents never go anywhere.

432
00:40:45,659 --> 00:40:49,026
At least you have the house to yourselves.

433
00:40:51,098 --> 00:40:53,623
Where are you going?

434
00:40:54,635 --> 00:41:00,505
- I gotta get out of these clothes.
- It'll be cold in your room. Come into mine.

435
00:41:27,935 --> 00:41:30,836
You can wear this.

436
00:41:31,972 --> 00:41:34,202
Thanks.

437
00:41:52,660 --> 00:41:54,719
I'm going to get a Coca.

438
00:41:54,795 --> 00:41:57,286
- Want one?
- OK.

439
00:42:47,214 --> 00:42:51,514
- Could you open the door? My hands are full.
- Just a second.

440
00:42:54,321 --> 00:42:56,755
- Why didn't you open the door?
- I was...

441
00:42:56,824 --> 00:42:59,156
I was dressing.

442
00:43:16,644 --> 00:43:19,579
What film?

443
00:43:25,619 --> 00:43:28,679
In what film does a chorus line dance like this

444
00:43:28,756 --> 00:43:32,385
with a singer in a fur coat?

445
00:43:32,459 --> 00:43:35,087
- I've seen this film?
- We saw it together.

446
00:43:35,162 --> 00:43:37,255
We did? Give me a clue.

447
00:43:37,331 --> 00:43:40,323
- Certainly not.
- Come on. Be a sport. The director's name.

448
00:43:40,401 --> 00:43:42,266
No.

449
00:43:43,304 --> 00:43:47,035
- Number of words in the title?
- I said no.

450
00:43:47,975 --> 00:43:50,136
The first letter of the first word.

451
00:43:50,210 --> 00:43:52,508
God. You're pathetic.

452
00:43:52,579 --> 00:43:55,605
Isn't he pathetic?
Don't you think he's pathetic?

453
00:43:55,683 --> 00:43:59,847
- Matthew, I bet you know.
- Don't you dare help him.

454
00:43:59,920 --> 00:44:02,684
Did the Sphinx give Oedipus a clue?

455
00:44:02,756 --> 00:44:04,917
Fuck you.

456
00:44:06,193 --> 00:44:08,218
Give up?

457
00:44:20,441 --> 00:44:21,738
<i>Blonde Venus.</i>

458
00:44:21,809 --> 00:44:22,434
Shit...

459
00:44:22,509 --> 00:44:25,137
Marlene Dietrich. 1932.

460
00:44:25,212 --> 00:44:27,874
I knew that.

461
00:44:29,016 --> 00:44:32,474
- Forfeit.
- If you insist.

462
00:44:32,553 --> 00:44:35,113
I dare you to do now,

463
00:44:35,189 --> 00:44:40,752
in front of us,
what I have watched you do in front of her.

464
00:44:40,828 --> 00:44:42,591
Who?

465
00:44:42,663 --> 00:44:47,691
- I don't know what you're talking about.
- Oh, yes, you do, my pet.

466
00:44:47,768 --> 00:44:49,167
Forfeit.

467
00:44:50,537 --> 00:44:54,371
What a bitch you are. A bitch and a sadist.

468
00:44:54,441 --> 00:44:57,842
Are you going to pay the forfeit or not?

469
00:45:04,051 --> 00:45:06,212
Very well.

470
00:45:14,928 --> 00:45:20,264
I want you to do it the way you did it
when you thought no one was watching.

471
00:47:06,907 --> 00:47:10,866
Let's have a drink. I'll meet you downstairs.

472
00:47:45,279 --> 00:47:50,080
Why won't you admit you were thrilled?
Come on, you can tell me.

473
00:47:51,752 --> 00:47:53,014
What?

474
00:47:53,086 --> 00:47:56,886
- Weren't you just a tiny bit excited?
- What is it you're trying to get me to say?

475
00:47:56,957 --> 00:47:59,653
Nothing. I asked you a question.

476
00:47:59,726 --> 00:48:03,059
- I suppose you know who you sound like.
- Who?

477
00:48:03,130 --> 00:48:05,963
- Isabelle.
- Why not? She's my twin sister.

478
00:48:06,033 --> 00:48:09,901
- You two are twins?
- Yes. She'd be me if she were a man.

479
00:48:09,970 --> 00:48:15,203
- That's crap. You're not identical.
- Yes, we are. We're Siamese twins.

480
00:48:15,275 --> 00:48:17,300
Joined here.

481
00:48:20,547 --> 00:48:22,742
You're a strange one, Theo.

482
00:48:22,816 --> 00:48:28,049
Half an hour ago, I saw you look at her
as though you wanted to strangle her.

483
00:48:28,121 --> 00:48:30,282
I did.

484
00:48:30,357 --> 00:48:33,520
- I don't understand you.
- Don't you have brothers or sisters?

485
00:48:33,594 --> 00:48:37,325
- I have two older sisters.
- Didn't you ever want to strangle them?

486
