- Just calm down. We hear you.

736
01:19:11,128 --> 01:19:13,619
Think about it. Think.

737
01:19:14,631 --> 01:19:17,759
You sleep in the same bed together.
Every night.

738
01:19:17,834 --> 01:19:20,735
You bathe together,
you pee in the john together.

739
01:19:20,804 --> 01:19:23,705
You play these little games.

740
01:19:23,774 --> 01:19:27,403
I wish you could step out of yourselves
and just look.

741
01:19:27,477 --> 01:19:30,310
Why? Why are you so cruel?

742
01:19:30,380 --> 01:19:33,611
- Because I love you.
- You have a strange way of showing it.

743
01:19:33,684 --> 01:19:36,653
No, I really love you. Both of you.

744
01:19:36,720 --> 01:19:38,984
And I admire you.

745
01:19:39,056 --> 01:19:42,856
And I look at you,
and I listen to you, and I think:

746
01:19:43,860 --> 01:19:49,093
"You're never gonna grow".
You won't grow like this. You won't.

747
01:19:49,166 --> 01:19:54,263
Not as long as you keep clinging
to each other the way that you do.

748
01:19:56,273 --> 01:19:59,709
- Isabelle, have you ever been on a date?
- A date?

749
01:19:59,776 --> 01:20:01,801
- Yeah, a date.
- What kind of date?

750
01:20:01,878 --> 01:20:04,938
- Have you ever been out with a boy?
- I've been out with Theo.

751
01:20:05,015 --> 01:20:07,279
Not Theo.

752
01:20:07,351 --> 01:20:10,343
Have you ever been out
with a boy from school that you liked?

753
01:20:10,420 --> 01:20:13,253
I was never taken to a school prom,
if that's what you mean.

754
01:20:13,323 --> 01:20:18,420
- I'm afraid we don't have proms in France.
- You have dates in France, Isabelle.

755
01:20:18,495 --> 01:20:21,487
Come on. Have you ever been out with a boy?

756
01:20:21,565 --> 01:20:24,728
Why do you keep asking me that?
You know I haven't.

757
01:20:24,801 --> 01:20:26,962
Would you like to?

758
01:20:27,037 --> 01:20:29,403
- Is this an invitation?
- It's an invitation.

759
01:20:29,473 --> 01:20:34,172
Would you like to go out on a date with me?
Just the two of us.

760
01:20:34,244 --> 01:20:36,439
Don't look at Theo.

761
01:20:36,513 --> 01:20:39,482
Isabelle, you don't need his permission.

762
01:21:13,250 --> 01:21:17,016
No. We can't sit at the front.
We have to sit at the back.

763
01:21:17,087 --> 01:21:19,988
The front is for people who don't have dates.

764
01:21:20,056 --> 01:21:22,752
We're on a... Sorry. Excuse me.

765
01:21:22,826 --> 01:21:28,822
On a date, you always sit at the back.
People sit at the back so they can make out.

766
01:21:43,079 --> 01:21:45,547
Ladies and gentlemen,

767
01:21:46,550 --> 01:21:51,749
the motion picture you are about to see
is a story of music.

768
01:21:51,822 --> 01:21:55,280
I play the role of Tom Miller, an agent,

769
01:21:55,358 --> 01:21:58,725
a small-time theatrical agent
who had been a...

770
01:21:59,796 --> 01:22:02,492
Well, you'll see.

771
01:22:02,566 --> 01:22:06,332
This motion picture was photographed
in the grandeur of CinemaScope,

772
01:22:06,403 --> 01:22:07,961
and...

773
01:22:09,306 --> 01:22:12,002
Pardon me.

774
01:22:25,489 --> 01:22:29,619
Sorry I can't stay, but I have a train on tap.

775
01:22:30,627 --> 01:22:32,720
All the best, Georgie.

776
01:22:32,796 --> 01:22:37,597
Can't stand to hear me sing again?

777
01:22:39,102 --> 01:22:42,071
You know it isn't that.

778
01:23:55,045 --> 01:23:59,482
When I looked at the TV screen
I remembered the battle of the Cinémathèque.

779
01:23:59,549 --> 01:24:03,076
Except this time
the demonstrators weren't film buffs.

780
01:24:03,153 --> 01:24:06,316
They weren't even just students any longer.

781
01:24:07,824 --> 01:24:12,318
It was hard to figure out what was happening.
It looked like shops had closed their doors,

782
01:24:12,395 --> 01:24:18,334
factories had gone on strike,
and it was beginning to spread all over Paris.

783
01:24:19,235 --> 01:24:22,136
We're willing to consider

784
01:24:22,205 --> 01:24:25,003
all legitimate demands.

785
01:24:26,443 --> 01:24:30,345
Theo and I never watch television.
We are purists.

786
01:24:30,413 --> 01:24:32,278
The purest of the pure.

787
01:24:32,349 --> 01:24:35,341
- Let's go.
- Yeah, but...

788
01:24:37,253 --> 01:24:38,982
Jesus.

789
01:25:36,413 --> 01:25:38,881
- What's wrong?
- They're not mine.

790
01:25:47,490 --> 01:25:50,823
- No. Your room.
- No.

791
01:25:50,894 --> 01:25:56,764
I don't know how long I've been here,
but in all that time I haven't seen your room.

792
01:25:56,833 --> 01:25:59,893
- You do have a room, right?
- Yeah, of course I have.

793
01:25:59,969 --> 01:26:02,164
You don't think I always live in Theo's pigsty?

794
01:26:02,238 --> 01:26:04,297
How come we never go in there?

795
01:26:04,374 --> 01:26:07,571
No. No-one's making love on my bed.

796
01:26:07,644 --> 01:26:08,906
- Please.
- No.

797
01:26:08,978 --> 01:26:13,642
It's your room. It's part of you.
I wanna see it. Just tonight.

798
01:26:31,935 --> 01:26:35,496
I discovered a side of Isabelle
I'd never seen before.

799
01:26:35,572 --> 01:26:38,837
A secret side she hadn't wanted me to see.

800
01:26:39,909 --> 01:26:43,811
I suddenly thought of
my sisters' bedrooms in San Diego.

801
01:26:43,880 --> 01:26:48,749
I thought of our house and our neighbors'
houses, all alike with their green lawns,

802
01:26:48,818 --> 01:26:54,814
and their sprinklers, and their station wagons
parked outside the garage door.

803
01:27:44,908 --> 01:27:47,138
What sculpture?

804
01:27:50,547 --> 01:27:54,415
<i>I always wanted to make love
to the Venus de Milo.</i>

805
01:28:00,490 --> 01:28:04,256
I can't stop you. I've got no arms.

806
01:28:07,463 --> 01:28:10,227
I can't stop you.

807
01:28:47,003 --> 01:28:49,995
What's wrong? What's wrong?

808
01:28:51,274 --> 01:28:53,742
Don't. Don't.

809
01:28:55,445 --> 01:28:58,471
Don't listen to it.

810
01:28:58,548 --> 01:29:00,880
Don't cry.

811
01:29:04,420 --> 01:29:08,413
Please, leave me alone.
Leave me alone. Please.

812
01:29:09,425 --> 01:29:11,086
Please.

813
01:29:15,932 --> 01:29:19,265
- Go away!
- Isabelle.

814
01:29:19,335 --> 01:29:22,168
Theo, open up!

815
01:29:24,674 --> 01:29:28,075
You're gonna hurt yourself. It's OK.

816
01:29:28,144 --> 01:29:31,477
- Who are you?
- What? What are you talking about?

817
01:29:31,547 --> 01:29:34,311
- What are you doing in my room?
- What do you mean...

818
01:29:34,384 --> 01:29:37,217
Get out! Get out!

819
01:30:13,156 --> 01:30:15,624
A revolution isn't a gala dinner.

820
01:30:16,192 --> 01:30:20,424
It cannot be created
like a book, a drawing or a tapestry.

821
01:30:20,496 --> 01:30:24,762
It cannot unfold with such elegance,
tranquility and delicacy

822
01:30:24,834 --> 01:30:27,496
or with sweetness, affability, courtesy,

823
01:30:27,570 --> 01:30:30,539
restraint and generosity.

824
01:30:30,606 --> 01:30:32,597
A revolution is an uprising,

825
01:30:32,675 --> 01:30:34,506
a violent act

826
01:30:34,577 --> 01:30:37,910
by which one class
overthrows another.

827
01:31:25,828 --> 01:31:29,093
<i>Château Lafite, 1955...</i>

828
01:31:30,600 --> 01:31:35,435
<i>Château Chasse-Spleen, 1959...</i>

829
01:31:36,406 --> 01:31:40,706
<i>Grand vin, 1937.
Happy birthday, papa.</i>

830
01:31:43,312 --> 01:31:45,746
Again?

831
01:31:45,815 --> 01:31:48,181
Listen, Matthew.

832
01:31:48,251 --> 01:31:51,618
You're a big movie buff, right?

833
01:31:51,687 --> 01:31:55,282
Then why don't you think of Mao
as a great director,

834
01:31:56,292 --> 01:31:58,920
making a movie with a cast of millions?

835
01:31:59,996 --> 01:32:05,628
All those millions of Red Guards
marching together into the future

836
01:32:05,701 --> 01:32:08,727
<i>with the Little Red Book in their hands.</i>

837
01:32:10,006 --> 01:32:12,600
Books, not guns.

838
01:32:12,675 --> 01:32:14,404
Culture,

839
01:32:14,477 --> 01:32:16,445
not violence.

840
01:32:16,512 --> 01:32:20,676
Can't you see what a beautiful,
epic movie that would make?

841
01:32:21,284 --> 01:32:25,311
I guess, but it's easy to say
"books, not guns".

842
01:32:25,755 --> 01:32:29,452
But it's not true. It's not books, it's book.

843
01:32:29,525 --> 01:32:32,255
A book. Just one book.

844
01:32:32,328 --> 01:32:35,764
- Shut up. You sound just like my father.
- No. Listen to me.

845
01:32:35,832 --> 01:32:39,734
The Red Guards that you admire,

846
01:32:39,802 --> 01:32:41,963
they all carry the same book,

847
01:32:42,038 --> 01:32:47,101
they all sing the same songs,
they all parrot the same slogans.

848
01:32:47,176 --> 01:32:51,977
So, in this big, epic movie,

849
01:32:52,048 --> 01:32:55,142
everybody is an extra.

850
01:32:56,853 --> 01:33:00,050
That's scary to me. That gives me the creeps.

851
01:33:00,123 --> 01:33:06,528
I'm sorry to say it,
but for me there is a distinct contradiction.

852
01:33:07,063 --> 01:33:08,894
Why?

853
01:33:08,965 --> 01:33:15,632
Because if you really believed
what you were saying, you'd be out there.

854
01:33:15,705 --> 01:33:17,263
Where?

855
01:33:17,340 --> 01:33:21,106
- Out there, on the street.
- I don't know what you mean.

856
01:33:21,177 --> 01:33:27,013
Yes, you do. Something's going on out there.
Something that could be really important.

857
01:33:27,083 --> 01:33:31,918
Something that feels like
things could change. Even I get that.

858
01:33:31,988 --> 01:33:34,650
But you're not out there.
You're inside with me,

859
01:33:34,724 --> 01:33:38,285
drinking expensive wine, talking about film,

860
01:33:38,361 --> 01:33:40,955
talking about Maoism. Why?

861
01:33:41,030 --> 01:33:42,520
- That's enough.
- Why?

862
01:33:42,598 --> 01:33:45,192
- That's enough.
- Ask yourself why.

863
01:33:46,102 --> 01:33:49,663
Because I don't think you really believe it.
I think you buy the lamp,

864
01:33:49,739 --> 01:33:52,833
and you put up the posters, but it ain't...

865
01:33:52,909 --> 01:33:57,437
- I don't think you...
- Look, you speak too much.

866
01:34:00,583 --> 01:34:03,143
Theo, just listen to me. Listen to me.

867
01:34:03,219 --> 01:34:06,848
I think you prefer...

868
01:34:06,923 --> 01:34:09,448
I think you prefer when the...

869
01:34:11,494 --> 01:34:17,364
when the word "together"
means not "a million", just "two".

870
01:34:19,135 --> 01:34:20,397
Boys, boys.

871
01:34:20,469 --> 01:34:22,528
Or "three".

872
01:34:28,177 --> 01:34:31,010
- Come and join us.
- No, thank you, my sweet.

873
01:34:31,080 --> 01:34:33,105
It smells like a whorehouse.

874
01:34:33,182 --> 01:34:34,774
Thanks a lot.

875
01:34:34,850 --> 01:34:37,580
<i>I've got a surprise for you. In the salon.</i>

876
01:34:45,761 --> 01:34:48,321
I'm getting right in.

877
01:34:49,565 --> 01:34:53,057
- It's beautiful.
- Come on. Get in.

878
01:35:01,811 --> 01:35:04,905
We used to do this
when we were little.

879
01:35:08,117 --> 01:35:10,176
More wine?

880
01:35:11,754 --> 01:35:13,813
Thank you.

881
01:35:18,561 --> 01:35:21,121
God, your breath is foul.

882
01:35:21,197 --> 01:35:24,428
- I'm sorry.
- You're drunk.

883
01:35:24,500 --> 01:35:26,627
Yes, I'm drunk.

884
01:35:26,702 --> 01:35:28,602
And you're beautiful.

885
01:35:28,671 --> 01:35:32,334
And tomorrow morning,
I'll be sober

886
01:35:32,408 --> 01:35:36,139
but you'll still be beautiful.

887
01:35:39,548 --> 01:35:41,709
Go to sleep, Matthew.

888
01:35:46,756 --> 01:35:49,247
Is it time to sleep?

889
01:35:49,325 --> 01:35:50,417
Idiot.

890
01:35:50,493 --> 01:35:53,485
You can hardly keep
your eyes open.

891
01:36:01,003 --> 01:36:03,403
Night-night.

892
01:36:20,523 --> 01:36:22,514
Wake up.

893
01:36:24,860 --> 01:36:27,124
What is it?

894
01:36:27,196 --> 01:36:29,892
I want you to listen.

895
01:36:29,965 --> 01:36:31,956
- Why?
- Because.

896
01:36:36,806 --> 01:36:39,969
I love you. You know that?

897
01:36:40,042 --> 01:36:42,306
I love you too.

898
01:36:43,312 --> 01:36:46,076
You love me too? That's funny.

899
01:36:48,617 --> 01:36:51,245
Are you listening?

900
01:36:51,320 --> 01:36:54,016
It's forever, right?

901
01:36:55,725 --> 01:36:57,022
What's forever?

902
01:36:57,093 --> 01:36:59,527
The two of us.

903
01:36:59,595 --> 01:37:01,529
- Right?
- Yes.

904
01:37:02,965 --> 01:37:05,399
Why did Matthew say that?

905
01:37:05,468 --> 01:37:08,164
What did Matthew say?

906
01:37:08,237 --> 01:37:11,365
That we're monsters, freaks.

907
01:37:13,576 --> 01:37:17,569
I just want you to tell me
that it's forever.

908
01:37:17,646 --> 01:37:19,637
It's forever.

909
01:37:21,150 --> 01:37:22,742
You don't understand.

910
01:37:22,818 --> 01:37:25,582
We'll talk about it in the morning.

911
01:37:25,654 --> 01:37:27,645
I promise.

912
01:39:23,973 --> 01:39:28,376
- Good. We must leave now, darling.
- Already?

913
01:39:28,444 --> 01:39:31,845
What would you like?
To have dinner with them?

914
01:39:46,328 --> 01:39:48,819
We must leave on tiptoe.

915
01:45:13,455 --> 01:45:14,820
What's happening?

916
01:45:14,890 --> 01:45:19,156
- The street came flying into the room!
- What?

917
01:45:19,228 --> 01:45:22,197
- What's that smell?
- It's tear gas.

918
01:45:45,988 --> 01:45:47,512
Hit the streets!

919
01:45:47,589 --> 01:45:48,521
Go!

920
01:45:48,590 --> 01:45:50,581
Go on!

921
01:46:00,602 --> 01:46:03,503
Hit the streets!

922
01:46:41,743 --> 01:46:45,235
This is just the start,
the fight goes on!

923
01:47:00,896 --> 01:47:03,091
Theo, this is wrong. This is wrong.

924
01:47:03,165 --> 01:47:06,191
- This is wonderful, Matthew.
- This is violence.

925
01:47:06,268 --> 01:47:08,429
It's not violence. Come with me.

926
01:47:08,503 --> 01:47:10,767
This is fucking fascism in a fucking bottle.

927
01:47:10,839 --> 01:47:13,137
I'm no fascist! The cops are fascists!

928
01:47:13,208 --> 01:47:16,769
- Yeah, and police hit people.
- Shut up!

929
01:47:16,845 --> 01:47:19,313
You can't understand. Leave me be!

930
01:47:19,381 --> 01:47:23,545
Listen to me for a second, OK?
This is what they do. This is not what we do.

931
01:47:23,618 --> 01:47:27,782
We use this. We do this.
We use this. We use this.

932
01:47:27,856 --> 01:47:29,847
Stop it!

933
01:54:37,886 --> 01:54:41,378
Visiontext Subtitles: Joanna Wilkinson

934
01:54:52,767 --> 01:54:54,758
US ENGLISH
